  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 200 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nSynopsis: The cold front lingering to the north of the region will \nprogress through the area on Sunday, slowly washing out in the \nprocess. North-northeast winds will become established in the wake \nof the boundary, and will be reinforced into Monday as high pressure \nmoves into Ontario from the Canadian Prairies. Affiliated with upper \ntroughing currently moving into southern California, an upper wave \nwill slowly eject from the Rockies into the Northern Plains on \nMonday. The wave will deamplify as it tries to work toward northern \nWisconsin on Tuesday, decaying over the general vicinity Tuesday \nnight. Rain is possible in the vicinity of the upper wave Monday \nnight into Tuesday, particularly over the northwest half of the \narea. Uncertainty increases---both in deterministic and ensemble \nguidance---moving into the mid-late portions of next week, with an \nupper trough & affiliated surface front expected to move through the \nwestern Great Lakes at some point during the Wednesday-Friday time \nframe. Amplitude, strength, and precise passage timing of the \nfeatures remain uncertain, making it difficult to offer exact \ndetails regarding precipitation chances and any post-frontal \ntemperature drops at this time. Will continue to provide updates as \ntrends clarify over coming forecasts. \n\nMonday night into Tuesday: Will be monitoring for possible rain \nchances ahead of an approaching upper shortwave. General consensus \ndepicted in guidance is for the wave to induce weak surface \ncyclogenesis from the Dakotas into Iowa, with rain blossoming along \nthe northeast periphery of the surface low where 1) mid-level DPVA \nand 2) frontogenesis will be maximized in the 1000-700 mb layer. \nSome uncertainty remains regarding where exactly these forcing \nmechanisms will overlap, with the strength of high pressure in the \nOhio Valley being the primary northwest-southeast modulator of \nprecip placement. As of this forecast, appears that the northwest \nhalf of the area will be most favored for precip, as this is where \nseparation from the Ohio Valley high pressure will be greatest. \nBlended precip probability solution from the NBM thus appears \nreasonable at this time, and has been largely maintained in the \nafternoon update. Will continue to provide updates in coming \nforecasts.\n\nWednesday through Friday: Expecting that rain chances & cooler \ntemperatures will return areawide at some point as an upper trough \nand cold front move through the region. Primary question is that of \ntiming, with a number of different solutions evident in current \nglobal forecast guidance. As such, blended precip probabilities from \nthe current NBM depict a wide range of chance (~20-40%) rain \npotential during this portion of the period, when in reality a \nnarrower window of widespread rain potential will emerge as the \nforecast picture clarifies into next week. With a multitude of \npotential solutions in place & none more favored than the other, \nhave maintained the wider window of precip potential in the \nafternoon update. Will continue to monitor trends into next week.  \n\nQuigley",
